## When verification fails
Read `.ai/skills/fix-failing-gate/SKILL.md`. In short: reproduce, find the root cause, smallest fix in code, re-run the gate, then the full `verify`.
The loop stops on its own after `<%noProgressLimit%>` identical failures in a row (exit 3) or `<%maxIterations%>` iterations (exit 1): a human looks at it then.

## Agent integration
`./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc implement` and the healing loop run an agent: `.ai/sdlc.json` -> `agent.command`, a shell command that reads the prompt on stdin<%#agentConfigured%> (preset `<%agentPreset%>`, currently `<%agentCommand%>`)<%/agentConfigured%><%^agentConfigured%> (currently empty: the tool prints the prompt and waits for you)<%/agentConfigured%>.
An agent can also be a **desktop app**: `claude-desktop` runs the Claude Code that the Claude desktop app keeps for its Code tab (unattended, no CLI on PATH needed), and `handoff-claude`, `handoff-cursor` and `handoff-vscode` open that app on this project, put the instruction on the clipboard and wait for the person to press Enter before the tool verifies (a hand-off needs a terminal; it reads the same prompt file).
A person picks the agent for their machine with `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc agent` (saved in `.ai/agent.local.json`, not committed); per run: `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc implement FEAT-001 --agent <name>` or `SDLC_AGENT_CMD='<your-agent-cli>'`. Do not edit `.ai/sdlc.json` or `.ai/agent.local.json` yourself. Prompts: `.ai/prompts/` (`implement.md` is filled with the feature's prd.md and acceptance.json).
If you are the agent: after adding a dependency to a manifest run `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc sync`; never edit the spec; never commit or push; if the spec is unclear, write your questions to the file named in the prompt and stop.
If a person started you from a desktop app instead of `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc`, the same rules apply. Do not run `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc implement`, `spec`, `feature` or `loop` yourself: they start another agent or wait for a person (`implement FEAT-001 --dry-run` is fine: it runs nothing and only writes the prompt file). Use `gates`, `facts`, `verify` and `sync`, and report what you ran and what `.ai/evidence/latest/report.md` says. Never claim a pass you did not see.
In Claude Code the person starts a step with a slash command: `/sdlc-implement FEAT-001`, `/sdlc-spec <document>`, `/sdlc-new <name>`, `/sdlc-verify`, `/sdlc-gates`, `/sdlc-setup` or `/sdlc-doctor` (`.claude/skills/sdlc-*/SKILL.md`). Each walks you through exactly the rules above, in order: follow it when it is invoked.
Skills are plain Markdown in `.ai/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (frontmatter: name, description). To expose them to a tool that looks in another folder, link each skill there, e.g. `ln -s ../../.ai/skills/write-prd .claude/skills/write-prd` (Claude Code) or `.agents/skills/` (Codex); the README shows a loop that links them all.
